@vsm 0.3

// LEDGER-DEAF — diagnosis and root cause analysis
// Date: 2026-07-17 (session start), analysis completed 2026-07-17T03:59
// Agent: DeepSeek V4 (vsf-s1-executor)

fact("ledger-deaf-symptom") = {
  body:  "The algedonic ledger (state.db + ledger.db) stopped receiving S3* findings on 2026-07-11T21:30:33Z (last row in ledger.db). 164 signals across 6 families remained in pending_human with no new entries for 5 days.",
  why:   "This is the surface symptom — the real cause was originally thought to be the vsf-s5-ledger NATS_URL malformation (NATS auth failure preventing the ledger from receiving messages). Investigation revealed TWO independent faults.",
  apply: "Do not close this diagnosis until both faults are resolved and end-to-end verification shows a finding flowing from S3* → NATS → ledger.db",
}

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// FAULT 1 — vsf-s5-ledger: stale process with original service file
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

fact("fault-1-ledger-stale-process") = {
  body:  "vsf-s5-ledger.service on LXC 104 had Environment=NATS_URL=nats://TOKEN:TOKEN@10.10.20.20:4222 (token duplicated) since the service file was written. On 2026-07-12T01:11 the file was corrected to single-token, but the vsf-s5-ledger (PID 94505) was NEVER restarted — it kept running with the old in-memory malformed URL and could not authenticate with NATS. The NATS log shows `authentication error` from 10.10.20.104 every 5 seconds from 2026-07-11T23:33:10 until I killed PID 94505 on 2026-07-17T03:31.",
  why:   "A service file fix is invisible until the service is restarted. An R4 violation (verify before mutating): the fix was applied but never verified to take effect.",
  apply: "When fixing a systemd service file: restart immediately and verify (systemctl is-active + journalctl for the new PID). Document the restart in the session block.",
}

fact("fault-1-ledger-code-uses-file-not-env") = {
  body:  "The vsf-s5-ledger Python code (`/usr/local/bin/vsf_s5_ledger.py`) reads the NATS token from `/etc/vsf/nats.token` file on disk, NOT from the `NATS_URL` environment variable. The `NATS_URL` env var is set in the systemd service file but never referenced in the code. The code uses `NATS_HOST` and `NATS_PORT` for the TCP connection, and reads the token separately from `/etc/vsf/nats.token`.",
  why:   "This means the NATS_URL fix (removing the duplicate token) had NO EFFECT on the connection. The real connection code was always correct — it just needed the token file and a service restart. The fix I applied (correcting the URL) was cosmetic only.",
  apply: "Do not rely on environment variable documentation in service files as authoritative. Read the actual code to understand how configuration is loaded.",
}

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// FAULT 2 — S3* (vsf-s3-star): algedonic findings not published to NATS
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

fact("fault-2-s3star-publish-silent-fail") = {
  body:  "After restarting vsf-s5-ledger (PID 141327, connected 2026-07-17T03:32:00), the NATS connection shows in_msgs=0 after 25+ minutes of uptime, with 3 subscriptions confirmed (vsf.alpha.algedonic.>, vsf.alpha.s5.covenant_halt, vsf.alpha.s3_star.audit.>). Direct NATS subscription to these subjects for 15 seconds confirmed zero messages flowing. Meanwhile, the S3* (vsf-s3-star, PID 124003, LXC 107) continues to generate findings every ~17 minutes (journal shows FIRED merkle_break at 03:31:13 and 03:48:53, with transport=nats) and writes them to events.jsonl on LXC 107 — but the findings NEVER appear on any NATS subject.",
  why:   "The S3* binary (Rust, async-nats 0.49.1) has a bug where its algedonic publish call fails silently. The `[s3_star.algedonic] FIRED ... (transport=nats)` log message is emitted BEFORE the actual publish call (or the publish failure is not logged). This means the algedonic channel from S3* to the ledger is broken at the PUBLISH level, not the SUBSCRIBE level.",
  apply: "The S3* code needs to be inspected — the publish call may be using a different NATS connection (short-lived) or the async-nats publish may fail without error propagation.",
}

fact("fault-2-s3star-publish-subject-mismatch-ruled-out") = {
  body:  "Direct NATS subscription to vsf.alpha.> (all subjects) for 15 seconds at 03:58:40 received TWO messages, both vsf.alpha.vsf_nucleus.up.* (kernel_tick, invariant_check). Zero messages received on vsf.alpha.algedonic.>, vsf.alpha.s3_star.audit.>, or any other non-nucleus subject. This rules out a subject mismatch — S3* is simply not publishing anything to NATS.",
  why:   "Nucleus events DO flow through NATS (kernel_tick arrives every 60s), confirming the NATS infrastructure is healthy. The S3* can read from NATS (its ingest receives via vsf.alpha.vsf_nucleus.up.>) but cannot write back.",
  apply: "The bug is in the S3* publish path specifically. Focus debugging on the Rust async-nats publish call in vsf-s3-star source (projects/vsf-nucleus/src/).",
}

